      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330022733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "REST & HTTP Caching Architecture",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "REST & HTTP Caching Architecture",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "l3wBhzOzHA9hwQSbziDut",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 268.4635025653831,
      "y": 862.2226475281478,
      "width": 708.8792724609375,
      "height": 740,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "az",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1509174011,
      "version": 474,
      "versionNonce": 2047364981,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330022733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "REST caching relies entirely on native HTTP headers rather than custom backend logic. \nThe goal is to eliminate network trips and backend processing for read-heavy,\n repetitive requests.\n\n1. The Two Caching Paradigms\nExpiration (Freshness): Dictated by the Cache-Control: max-age=X header. \nThe client reads the data directly from its local memory for the specified duration. \nZero network requests are made.\n\nValidation (Revalidation): Dictated by the ETag header (a unique hash of the data). \nWhen the cache expires, the client makes a network request with If-None-Match: .\n\nCache Hit: Server calculates the current hash. \nIf it matches, it returns a 304 Not Modified (empty body, resets the timer).\n\nCache Miss: Data has changed. Server returns a 200 OK with the new payload \nand a new ETag.\n\n2. The Three Caching Tiers\nClient Cache (Browser/Mobile): Lives on the user's device. Strictly for private, \nsingle-user data.\n\nShared Edge Cache (CDN): Geographically distributed servers (like Cloudflare edge nodes). \nIntercepts global requests locally to drastically reduce latency for public, static data.\n\nGateway Cache (Reverse Proxy): Systems like Nginx or Varnish sitting directly in front of\n your origin servers. Protects your databases and API code from repetitive queries.\n\n3. CDN Strategy: Build vs. Buy\nBuy (Cloudflare, AWS CloudFront): Used by 99% of companies. \nProvides instant global edge presence, DDoS protection, \nand immediate latency reduction with zero hardware maintenance.\n\nBuild (Custom Private CDNs): Used by massive-scale companies (Netflix Open Connect, \nGoogle Global Cache, Meta FNA). They deploy proprietary hardware directly into local \nInternet Service Providers (ISPs) to avoid third-party bandwidth costs and optimize for\nspecific traffic like heavy video streaming.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "REST caching relies entirely on native HTTP headers rather than custom backend logic. \nThe goal is to eliminate network trips and backend processing for read-heavy,\n repetitive requests.\n\n1. The Two Caching Paradigms\nExpiration (Freshness): Dictated by the Cache-Control: max-age=X header. \nThe client reads the data directly from its local memory for the specified duration. \nZero network requests are made.\n\nValidation (Revalidation): Dictated by the ETag header (a unique hash of the data). \nWhen the cache expires, the client makes a network request with If-None-Match: .\n\nCache Hit: Server calculates the current hash. \nIf it matches, it returns a 304 Not Modified (empty body, resets the timer).\n\nCache Miss: Data has changed. Server returns a 200 OK with the new payload \nand a new ETag.\n\n2. The Three Caching Tiers\nClient Cache (Browser/Mobile): Lives on the user's device. Strictly for private, \nsingle-user data.\n\nShared Edge Cache (CDN): Geographically distributed servers (like Cloudflare edge nodes). \nIntercepts global requests locally to drastically reduce latency for public, static data.\n\nGateway Cache (Reverse Proxy): Systems like Nginx or Varnish sitting directly in front of\n your origin servers. Protects your databases and API code from repetitive queries.\n\n3. CDN Strategy: Build vs. Buy\nBuy (Cloudflare, AWS CloudFront): Used by 99% of companies. \nProvides instant global edge presence, DDoS protection, \nand immediate latency reduction with zero hardware maintenance.\n\nBuild (Custom Private CDNs): Used by massive-scale companies (Netflix Open Connect, \nGoogle Global Cache, Meta FNA). They deploy proprietary hardware directly into local \nInternet Service Providers (ISPs) to avoid third-party bandwidth costs and optimize for\nspecific traffic like heavy video streaming.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "s1C7s4iUu-vrK4htYhjDC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 473.39807426676043,
      "y": 1609.559809829732,
      "width": 209.93991088867188,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b02",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1057638165,
      "version": 532,
      "versionNonce": 1268291797,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330022733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "REST LIMITATIONS",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "REST LIMITATIONS",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wtfLQ7uFpCB8MIDXpSTNA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 260.6713862915635,
      "y": 1641.5598504996367,
      "width": 710.3995361328125,
      "height": 620,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b05",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1726783131,
      "version": 372,
      "versionNonce": 186168885,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330022733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Over-fetching & Under-fetching (Data Shape): Fixed endpoints force clients to download\nunnecessary data (over-fetching) or make multiple round-trips to assemble related data\n(under-fetching/N+1 problem).\n\nAlternative: GraphQL (clients request exactly the fields they need in a single query).\n\nReal-Time Data Inefficiency: REST relies on a client-initiated request-response model. \nGetting live updates requires continuous, resource-heavy polling.\n\nAlternatives: WebSockets (bidirectional, persistent connections), Server-Sent Events (SSE)\n (unidirectional server-to-client streaming).\n\nPerformance Overhead (Serialization): Parsing large, text-based JSON payloads over \nHTTP/1.1 consumes high CPU cycles and network bandwidth, creating bottlenecks in \ninternal microservice communication.\n\nAlternative: gRPC (uses Protocol Buffers for lightweight, compressed binary serialization\nover HTTP/2).\n\nThe CRUD Trap (Complex Actions): REST forces all operations to map to resources \n(nouns) using standard HTTP verbs. Complex actions like \"TransferFunds\" or \n\"RestartServer\" don't map cleanly to standard CRUD operations.\n\nAlternative: RPC / gRPC (designed for action-oriented, procedure-based endpoints).\n\nLack of Native Type Safety: REST does not enforce strict data contracts natively. \nA server can silently change a field type (e.g., integer to string), causing client \napplications to crash at runtime.\n\nAlternatives: GraphQL and gRPC (both require strongly typed schema definitions that \nact as strict contracts between client and server).",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Over-fetching & Under-fetching (Data Shape): Fixed endpoints force clients to download\nunnecessary data (over-fetching) or make multiple round-trips to assemble related data\n(under-fetching/N+1 problem).\n\nAlternative: GraphQL (clients request exactly the fields they need in a single query).\n\nReal-Time Data Inefficiency: REST relies on a client-initiated request-response model. \nGetting live updates requires continuous, resource-heavy polling.\n\nAlternatives: WebSockets (bidirectional, persistent connections), Server-Sent Events (SSE)\n (unidirectional server-to-client streaming).\n\nPerformance Overhead (Serialization): Parsing large, text-based JSON payloads over \nHTTP/1.1 consumes high CPU cycles and network bandwidth, creating bottlenecks in \ninternal microservice communication.\n\nAlternative: gRPC (uses Protocol Buffers for lightweight, compressed binary serialization\nover HTTP/2).\n\nThe CRUD Trap (Complex Actions): REST forces all operations to map to resources \n(nouns) using standard HTTP verbs. Complex actions like \"TransferFunds\" or \n\"RestartServer\" don't map cleanly to standard CRUD operations.\n\nAlternative: RPC / gRPC (designed for action-oriented, procedure-based endpoints).\n\nLack of Native Type Safety: REST does not enforce strict data contracts natively. \nA server can silently change a field type (e.g., integer to string), causing client \napplications to crash at runtime.\n\nAlternatives: GraphQL and gRPC (both require strongly typed schema definitions that \nact as strict contracts between client and server).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hL2TstXtamTqIWo0aPBUz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 266.7367129154245,
      "y": 2280.7251130441564,
      "width": 677.0394287109375,
      "height": 60,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1262324469,
      "version": 423,
      "versionNonce": 325986197,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330022733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Versioning complexity: When you need to change an endpoint, you either break existing \nclients or maintain multiple versions forever. /v1/users vs /v2/users with subtle \ndifferences creates perpetual maintenance burden.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Versioning complexity: When you need to change an endpoint, you either break existing \nclients or maintain multiple versions forever. /v1/users vs /v2/users with subtle \ndifferences creates perpetual maintenance burden.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PP7GSMU-KkW0IN0i5dneH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 261.07141070562625,
      "y": 2355.5053709664135,
      "width": 694.6873779296875,
      "height": 80,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 109074203,
      "version": 456,
      "versionNonce": 1960850933,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790334892774,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Alternative: GraphQL (promotes a \"versionless\" API model. Instead of versioning the \nentire endpoint, you continuously evolve a single schema by adding new fields and tagging\nold ones as @deprecated, allowing clients\nto migrate gradually without breaking).",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Alternative: GraphQL (promotes a \"versionless\" API model. Instead of versioning the \nentire endpoint, you continuously evolve a single schema by adding new fields and tagging\nold ones as @deprecated, allowing clients\nto migrate gradually without breaking).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
