      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790330179852,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "RPC/gRPC",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "y1PBOs6u5eK7h0qUqWUQR",
      "originalText": "RPC/gRPC",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vMqcSW4um_2dl24XZKnvk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1261.3343817677953,
      "y": -389.30401936888916,
      "width": 854.399169921875,
      "height": 2260,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 956203509,
      "version": 362,
      "versionNonce": 1433861947,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790335257776,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "RPC (Remote Procedure Call) has existed since the 1980s. The idea: make calling a function on a remote\nmachine look and feel exactly like calling a local function. The network communication is abstracted away.\nThe network call is hidden. You write `paymentService.processPayment()` and it feels like calling a local \nfunction. The RPC framework handles serialization, network transport, deserialization, and error handling.\n\nProtocol Buffers — What They Are and Why They Matter\n\nProtocol Buffers (protobuf) is gRPC's serialization format. Understanding it is the key to understanding \nwhy gRPC is fast.\n\nJSON (what REST uses):\n\njson\n{\n  \"userId\": \"12345\",\n  \"amount\": 99.99,\n  \"currency\": \"GBP\",\n  \"description\": \"Monthly subscription\"\n}\n\nThis JSON is:\n\nHuman readable\nText-based\n~80 bytes\nMust be parsed character by character\n\nProtobuf equivalent:\n\nprotobuf\nmessage PaymentRequest {\n    string user_id = 1;\n    double amount = 2;\n    string currency = 3;\n    string description = 4;\n}\n\nWhen serialized, this becomes approximately 35-40 bytes of binary data. The =1, =2, =3 are field numbers —\ninstead of sending the string \"user_id\" in every message, protobuf sends a 1-byte field number. \nThe receiving side knows field 1 is user_id because both sides compiled the same .proto file.\n\nWhy gRPC Uses HTTP/2\n\nREST runs on HTTP/1.1. HTTP/1.1 has a fundamental problem: head-of-line blocking. Each request-response \npair must complete before the next one starts on the same connection. \nBrowsers work around this by opening 6 parallel connections per domain — a hack.\n\nHTTP/2 introduces multiplexing: multiple requests and responses can be in flight simultaneously on a single \nconnection. Request 1, 2, and 3 can all be in flight at the same time. Response 2 can come back before \nResponse 1. No blocking.\n\ngRPC runs on HTTP/2, giving it:\n\n- Multiplexed requests — no head-of-line blocking\n- Header compression — HTTP headers are repeated in every request; HTTP/2 compresses them\n- Binary framing — more efficient than text-based HTTP/1.1\n- Native streaming — built into the protocol\n\ngRPC Service Definition Example Code\n\nFrom this single .proto file, gRPC generates:\n\nType-safe client code in Go, Python, Java, JavaScript, Ruby, C++, Swift, Kotlin...\nType-safe server interface code in the same languages\nSerialization/deserialization code\nDocumentation (via protoc-gen-doc)\n\nEvery team that needs to call PaymentService gets a generated client. They don't write HTTP calls. They \ndon't write JSON parsing. They call paymentService.processPayment(request) in their language and it works.\n\nThe Four gRPC Streaming Patterns:\n- Unary (standard request-response): Equivalent to a REST POST. Use this for most operations.\n- Server streaming: Client subscribes to a stream. Server sends data as it's available. Use for: live market \n  data, log streaming, progress updates on long operations. Twitter used this for streaming timelines.\n- Client Streaming: Both sides are streaming simultaneously on the same connection. Use for: real-time \n  collaboration, live chat, multiplayer gaming, interactive dashboards. Google used this in Google Docs for\n  real-time collaborative editing.\n- Bidirectional streaming: Both sides are streaming simultaneously on the same connection. Use for: real-time \n  collaboration, live chat, multiplayer gaming, interactive dashboards. Google used this in Google Docs for \n  real-time collaborative editing.\n\n                                        Status codes\nOK (0)                 — success\nCANCELLED (1)          — operation cancelled by client\nUNKNOWN (2)            — unknown error\nINVALID_ARGUMENT (3)   — bad input (like 400)\nNOT_FOUND (5)          — resource not found (like 404)\nALREADY_EXISTS (6)     — conflict (like 409)\nPERMISSION_DENIED (7)  — no permission (like 403)\nUNAUTHENTICATED (16)   — not authenticated (like 401)\nRESOURCE_EXHAUSTED (8) — rate limited (like 429)\nUNAVAILABLE (14)       — server temporarily unavailable (like 503)\nINTERNAL (13)          — server error (like 500)\n\n                                        gRPC Limitations\n\nBrowser Support (The Proxy Tax): Browsers completely abstract away HTTP/2 management for security \nand stability. They do not allow frontend JavaScript (like fetch() or XMLHttpRequest) to directly access \nthe raw HTTP/2 frames or \"trailing headers\" that gRPC relies on. To make a web frontend talk to a gRPC \nbackend, you must deploy an additional infrastructure component (a proxy like Envoy) and use a specialized \nlibrary (gRPC-Web) to translate normal web requests into gRPC streams.\n\nDebugging (The Visibility Problem): With REST, data is sent as plain text. If an endpoint is returning the \nwrong data, you can open your browser's Network tab or run a standard curl command and instantly read \n{ \"error\": \"missing id\" }. gRPC sends compressed binary data (e.g., \\x0A\\x0E\\n\\x0Cmissing id). You cannot \nsimply look at a payload to debug it; you must rely on specialized tools like grpcurl that require your .proto \nfile to decode the binary back into text.\n\nOverkill for Simple Services (The Boilerplate Tax): REST allows you to spin up an endpoint in seconds by \nsimply defining a route and returning an object. gRPC requires a heavy development pipeline: you must write a\n strict .proto schema, run a compiler to generate native classes/interfaces, and import that generated code \ninto your business logic. For a small CRUD API where performance bottlenecks aren't an issue, this mandat- \nory boilerplate dramatically slows down development speed.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "RPC (Remote Procedure Call) has existed since the 1980s. The idea: make calling a function on a remote\nmachine look and feel exactly like calling a local function. The network communication is abstracted away.\nThe network call is hidden. You write `paymentService.processPayment()` and it feels like calling a local \nfunction. The RPC framework handles serialization, network transport, deserialization, and error handling.\n\nProtocol Buffers — What They Are and Why They Matter\n\nProtocol Buffers (protobuf) is gRPC's serialization format. Understanding it is the key to understanding \nwhy gRPC is fast.\n\nJSON (what REST uses):\n\njson\n{\n  \"userId\": \"12345\",\n  \"amount\": 99.99,\n  \"currency\": \"GBP\",\n  \"description\": \"Monthly subscription\"\n}\n\nThis JSON is:\n\nHuman readable\nText-based\n~80 bytes\nMust be parsed character by character\n\nProtobuf equivalent:\n\nprotobuf\nmessage PaymentRequest {\n    string user_id = 1;\n    double amount = 2;\n    string currency = 3;\n    string description = 4;\n}\n\nWhen serialized, this becomes approximately 35-40 bytes of binary data. The =1, =2, =3 are field numbers —\ninstead of sending the string \"user_id\" in every message, protobuf sends a 1-byte field number. \nThe receiving side knows field 1 is user_id because both sides compiled the same .proto file.\n\nWhy gRPC Uses HTTP/2\n\nREST runs on HTTP/1.1. HTTP/1.1 has a fundamental problem: head-of-line blocking. Each request-response \npair must complete before the next one starts on the same connection. \nBrowsers work around this by opening 6 parallel connections per domain — a hack.\n\nHTTP/2 introduces multiplexing: multiple requests and responses can be in flight simultaneously on a single \nconnection. Request 1, 2, and 3 can all be in flight at the same time. Response 2 can come back before \nResponse 1. No blocking.\n\ngRPC runs on HTTP/2, giving it:\n\n- Multiplexed requests — no head-of-line blocking\n- Header compression — HTTP headers are repeated in every request; HTTP/2 compresses them\n- Binary framing — more efficient than text-based HTTP/1.1\n- Native streaming — built into the protocol\n\ngRPC Service Definition Example Code\n\nFrom this single .proto file, gRPC generates:\n\nType-safe client code in Go, Python, Java, JavaScript, Ruby, C++, Swift, Kotlin...\nType-safe server interface code in the same languages\nSerialization/deserialization code\nDocumentation (via protoc-gen-doc)\n\nEvery team that needs to call PaymentService gets a generated client. They don't write HTTP calls. They \ndon't write JSON parsing. They call paymentService.processPayment(request) in their language and it works.\n\nThe Four gRPC Streaming Patterns:\n- Unary (standard request-response): Equivalent to a REST POST. Use this for most operations.\n- Server streaming: Client subscribes to a stream. Server sends data as it's available. Use for: live market \n  data, log streaming, progress updates on long operations. Twitter used this for streaming timelines.\n- Client Streaming: Both sides are streaming simultaneously on the same connection. Use for: real-time \n  collaboration, live chat, multiplayer gaming, interactive dashboards. Google used this in Google Docs for\n  real-time collaborative editing.\n- Bidirectional streaming: Both sides are streaming simultaneously on the same connection. Use for: real-time \n  collaboration, live chat, multiplayer gaming, interactive dashboards. Google used this in Google Docs for \n  real-time collaborative editing.\n\n                                        Status codes\nOK (0)                 — success\nCANCELLED (1)          — operation cancelled by client\nUNKNOWN (2)            — unknown error\nINVALID_ARGUMENT (3)   — bad input (like 400)\nNOT_FOUND (5)          — resource not found (like 404)\nALREADY_EXISTS (6)     — conflict (like 409)\nPERMISSION_DENIED (7)  — no permission (like 403)\nUNAUTHENTICATED (16)   — not authenticated (like 401)\nRESOURCE_EXHAUSTED (8) — rate limited (like 429)\nUNAVAILABLE (14)       — server temporarily unavailable (like 503)\nINTERNAL (13)          — server error (like 500)\n\n                                        gRPC Limitations\n\nBrowser Support (The Proxy Tax): Browsers completely abstract away HTTP/2 management for security \nand stability. They do not allow frontend JavaScript (like fetch() or XMLHttpRequest) to directly access \nthe raw HTTP/2 frames or \"trailing headers\" that gRPC relies on. To make a web frontend talk to a gRPC \nbackend, you must deploy an additional infrastructure component (a proxy like Envoy) and use a specialized \nlibrary (gRPC-Web) to translate normal web requests into gRPC streams.\n\nDebugging (The Visibility Problem): With REST, data is sent as plain text. If an endpoint is returning the \nwrong data, you can open your browser's Network tab or run a standard curl command and instantly read \n{ \"error\": \"missing id\" }. gRPC sends compressed binary data (e.g., \\x0A\\x0E\\n\\x0Cmissing id). You cannot \nsimply look at a payload to debug it; you must rely on specialized tools like grpcurl that require your .proto \nfile to decode the binary back into text.\n\nOverkill for Simple Services (The Boilerplate Tax): REST allows you to spin up an endpoint in seconds by \nsimply defining a route and returning an object. gRPC requires a heavy development pipeline: you must write a\n strict .proto schema, run a compiler to generate native classes/interfaces, and import that generated code \ninto your business logic. For a small CRUD API where performance bottlenecks aren't an issue, this mandat- \nory boilerplate dramatically slows down development speed.",
