      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790385061880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Concepts and Notes:\n- Head-of-line (HOL) blocking is a performance bottleneck in queuing systems where a slow, \n  large, or lost packet at the front of a line prevents everything behind it from moving \n  forward, even if those subsequent items are ready to go.\n- gRPC is locked to HTTP/2 by design, while REST is flexible and gets a free performance\n  boost whenever you upgrade your underlying web server infrastructure.\n- GraphQL (Frontend-to-Backend): Designed for the \"last mile\" between the server and the\n  user's device. It solves over-fetching by letting the client dictate the payload, optimizing \n  for slow, unpredictable mobile networks and diverse UI requirements.\n- gRPC (Backend-to-Backend): Designed for internal microservice communication. Because \n  internal data centers have massive bandwidth, over-fetching a few extra fields is not a \n  major bottleneck. Instead, gRPC prioritizes raw serialization speed, strict type safety, \n  and low CPU overhead\n- gRPC (The .proto contract): The schema is the absolute source of truth. If the server \n  defines a user_id as an integer and the client tries to pass a string, the generated code \n  will fail to compile. The contract is strictly enforced at the binary and application-code level.\n- GraphQL (The SDL contract): The server defines its capabilities using the Schema Definition\n  Language (e.g., type User { id: Int! }). When a client sends a query, the GraphQL engine \n  validates the request against the schema before it ever hits the database. If a requested \n  field doesn't exist, it rejects the query immediately with a strict schema error.\n- REST (Schema-less by default): Natively, REST is just sending arbitrary JSON over HTTP. \n  A backend developer can silently change {\"age\": 25} to {\"age\": \"twenty-five\"}, and the client \n  application will simply crash at runtime when it tries to parse it. To get schema enforcement \n  in REST, you have to manually integrate external tools like OpenAPI (Swagger) for documen-\n  tation and runtime validation libraries like Zod or Joi in your server code.\n- Why gRPC does NOT solve N+1: gRPC relies on rigid, server-defined methods (just like REST\n  endpoints). If your .proto file has a GetUsers() method and a GetOrders(userID) method, \n  the client still has to make 1 initial call for the users, and N additional calls to get their \n  individual orders. The only way to avoid this in gRPC is for the backend engineer to manually\n  write a brand new, highly specific method like GetUsersWithOrders().\n- Why GraphQL solves N+1 (Network Layer): GraphQL allows the client to traverse relational \n  graphs dynamically. A client can send a single query asking for a list of users and their \n  nested orders in one request. The GraphQL server does the heavy lifting to aggregate this \n  and returns it in one single network payload, completely eliminating the extra client-server \n  round trips.\n- While GraphQL brilliantly solves the Network N+1 problem (saving the mobile app from making \n  50 API calls), it can accidentally cause a massive Database N+1 problem. Because GraphQL \n  resolves fields individually on the server, a naive implementation might make 1 query to get 50\n  users, and then hit the database 50 separate times to get their orders. To fix this backend\n  bottleneck in GraphQL, developers have to use a batching tool like DataLoader.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Concepts and Notes:\n- Head-of-line (HOL) blocking is a performance bottleneck in queuing systems where a slow, \n  large, or lost packet at the front of a line prevents everything behind it from moving \n  forward, even if those subsequent items are ready to go.\n- gRPC is locked to HTTP/2 by design, while REST is flexible and gets a free performance\n  boost whenever you upgrade your underlying web server infrastructure.\n- GraphQL (Frontend-to-Backend): Designed for the \"last mile\" between the server and the\n  user's device. It solves over-fetching by letting the client dictate the payload, optimizing \n  for slow, unpredictable mobile networks and diverse UI requirements.\n- gRPC (Backend-to-Backend): Designed for internal microservice communication. Because \n  internal data centers have massive bandwidth, over-fetching a few extra fields is not a \n  major bottleneck. Instead, gRPC prioritizes raw serialization speed, strict type safety, \n  and low CPU overhead\n- gRPC (The .proto contract): The schema is the absolute source of truth. If the server \n  defines a user_id as an integer and the client tries to pass a string, the generated code \n  will fail to compile. The contract is strictly enforced at the binary and application-code level.\n- GraphQL (The SDL contract): The server defines its capabilities using the Schema Definition\n  Language (e.g., type User { id: Int! }). When a client sends a query, the GraphQL engine \n  validates the request against the schema before it ever hits the database. If a requested \n  field doesn't exist, it rejects the query immediately with a strict schema error.\n- REST (Schema-less by default): Natively, REST is just sending arbitrary JSON over HTTP. \n  A backend developer can silently change {\"age\": 25} to {\"age\": \"twenty-five\"}, and the client \n  application will simply crash at runtime when it tries to parse it. To get schema enforcement \n  in REST, you have to manually integrate external tools like OpenAPI (Swagger) for documen-\n  tation and runtime validation libraries like Zod or Joi in your server code.\n- Why gRPC does NOT solve N+1: gRPC relies on rigid, server-defined methods (just like REST\n  endpoints). If your .proto file has a GetUsers() method and a GetOrders(userID) method, \n  the client still has to make 1 initial call for the users, and N additional calls to get their \n  individual orders. The only way to avoid this in gRPC is for the backend engineer to manually\n  write a brand new, highly specific method like GetUsersWithOrders().\n- Why GraphQL solves N+1 (Network Layer): GraphQL allows the client to traverse relational \n  graphs dynamically. A client can send a single query asking for a list of users and their \n  nested orders in one request. The GraphQL server does the heavy lifting to aggregate this \n  and returns it in one single network payload, completely eliminating the extra client-server \n  round trips.\n- While GraphQL brilliantly solves the Network N+1 problem (saving the mobile app from making \n  50 API calls), it can accidentally cause a massive Database N+1 problem. Because GraphQL \n  resolves fields individually on the server, a naive implementation might make 1 query to get 50\n  users, and then hit the database 50 separate times to get their orders. To fix this backend\n  bottleneck in GraphQL, developers have to use a batching tool like DataLoader.\n",
